<div style="background-color: #1a5276; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: white; text-align: center; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Routines – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: white; text-align: center; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Pick It Up!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Engage with simple commands using basic words and gestures.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can name daily activities, using isolated words (e.g., 'sit' or 'stand').
Can follow and give very simple commands for interactions (e.g., 'Stand up!' 'I stand up!').
Can identify very simple classroom commands (e.g., 'Stand up' and 'sit down').
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using familiar classroom commands and actions. For example: "Simon says, 'Stand up!'," "Simon says, 'Sit down!'," "Simon says, 'Clap!'," "Simon says, 'Jump!'." Students respond with the corresponding actions. This activates prior knowledge of routines and movements.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher models the target commands clearly, emphasizing pronunciation of key sounds like /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down'. Teacher demonstrates actions for "Pick it up," "Put it down," "Stand up," and "Sit down" using real objects like a pen and paper. Teacher says, "Pick up the pen!" and picks up a pen. Then, "Put down the pen!" and puts it down.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a pen) "Is this 'pick up' or 'put down'?" (Students point or say the word).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Performing a sitting action) "Am I standing up or sitting down?" (Students say 'sitting down').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Routines")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A series of large, colorful flashcards depicting various classroom routine actions (e.g., a child standing, a child sitting, a child picking up a pen, a child picking up paper, a child putting a bag on a chair). Each card has a number (Step 1, Step 2, etc.) to introduce sequencing.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Following instructions and understanding daily routines. Students recognize and respond to simple classroom commands using gestures and isolated words.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows flashcards one by one, clearly stating the action and the step number. "Step 1: Stand up!" (shows child standing). "Step 2: Sit down!" (shows child sitting). "Step 3: Pick up the pen!" (shows child picking up a pen).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages choral repetition of the command and action. "What is Step 1?" (Students: "Stand up!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a flashcard and asks students to perform the action. "Show me 'Pick up the paper'!" (Students perform).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phonemic awareness focus: Teacher points to words starting with A, B, C, D, E on an alphabet chart or flashcards while saying the letter sound. For example, "A for Apple /æ/," "D for Down /d/."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students practice giving and following simple imperative commands using target vocabulary: 'sit', 'stand', 'pick up', 'put', 'pen', 'paper', 'desk', 'chair', 'bag', 'Please', 'up', 'down'. Emphasis on pronunciation of /p/ and /d/ sounds.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - TPR Drill):</b> Teacher gives commands and students perform actions. "Stand up!" "Sit down!" "Pick up your pen!" "Put down your paper!" Teacher ensures correct pronunciation and action. Repeat chorally and individually. Focus on /p/ in 'pick' and 'pen', and /d/ in 'down' and 'desk'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Response Drill):</b> Teacher gives a command (e.g., "Stand up!"). Students perform the action and echo the command in a short phrase: "I stand up!" Teacher: "Pick up the paper!" Students: "I pick up the paper!" Introduce "Please": Teacher: "Please, pick up your bag!" Students: "I pick up my bag."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Command Relay):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair has a small collection of classroom items (e.g., a pen, a paper, an eraser). Student A gives a command to Student B: "Please, pick up the pen." Student B performs the action and then gives a command to Student A: "Please, put down the eraser." They take turns practicing commands with "Please" and the target vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Routines")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Task: "Classroom Helper Routine"</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher designates one student as the "Classroom Helper" who will give commands to the others for a simple cleanup routine after an activity (e.g., after a drawing activity, there might be paper scraps and crayons). </p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher places various classroom items (paper, pens, erasers) on a table or on the floor.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Classroom Helper" gives commands to their group members to tidy up. Examples: "Please, pick up the paper." "Put the pen on the desk." "Pick up your bag."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Group members follow the commands and perform the actions. The helper encourages polite language by reminding "Say 'Please'!" if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, observing interactions and providing support. Groups take turns being the "Classroom Helper."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity directly integrates the theme "Pick It Up!" and the scenario "Our Routines," focusing on students actively speaking to give and follow instructions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">During the "Classroom Helper Routine," the teacher observes students' ability to follow and give simple commands, using targeted vocabulary and politeness markers. Teacher can use a simple checklist to note participation and accuracy.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /p/ in 'pick' and /d/ in 'down'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'sit', 'stand', 'pick up', 'pen', 'paper', 'Please').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Follows multi-step simple commands with correct actions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to give simple commands to a peer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses 'Please' when giving commands.</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Can you say 'Pick it up'?" (Students give a thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks: "Can you follow 'Sit down'?" (Students give a thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Student and Teacher reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompts:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new actions can you tell your friend to do?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it fun to 'Pick it up' and 'Put it down'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting speaking for Kinder students? (e.g., TPR, pair work, using real objects).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific vocabulary or commands were challenging for students to produce or follow? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more visual aids, slower pace, simpler commands).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the phonemic awareness activities effective? How can I reinforce /p/ and /d/ sounds?</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The next lesson will focus on Writing Composition. Students will use the vocabulary and commands practiced today (e.g., 'Pick it up', 'Sit down') to label pictures or write very short, simple sentences related to classroom routines, reinforcing the connection between action, word, and written form. We will build on the understanding of sequencing (Step 1, Step 2) to write simple instructions or descriptions of actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>